---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Agent Plugins compatibility"
  description="The Agent Plugins v1.0.0 compatibility target, current implementation status, and safety requirements for Veasel Code."
>
  <section class="doc-page page-grid">
    <p class="kicker">EXTENSIBILITY / COMPATIBILITY</p>
    <h1>
      One portable format.
      <br />
      <em>A careful host.</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Veasel Code targets the released Agent Plugins v1.0.0 specification. A
      compatible host must understand packages, Skills, and MCP configuration,
      then add its own clear trust and permission controls.
    </p>
    <div class="compatibility-banner">
      <span class="compatibility-mark" aria-hidden="true">
        ◌
      </span>
      <div>
        <strong>
          Experimental Skills support · no compatibility claim yet
        </strong>
        <p>
          Veasel discovers local packages and can activate validated Skills for
          one session. Skill instructions are bounded, reloaded for each turn,
          and treated as untrusted context. Veasel does not install packages,
          launch MCP servers, or grant Skills tool permissions.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>What the format brings</h2>
      <div class="guide-grid">
        <article class="guide-card">
          <span class="guide-card-mark">{}</span>
          <h3>Plugin package</h3>
          <p>
            A portable directory starts with a versioned{" "}
            <code>plugin.json</code> manifest.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="guide-card">
          <span class="guide-card-mark">✳</span>
          <h3>Agent Skills</h3>
          <p>
            Discover descriptions first; choose which bounded instruction body
            to include in a session.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="guide-card">
          <span class="guide-card-mark">⌘</span>
          <h3>MCP servers</h3>
          <p>
            Declare stdio or Streamable HTTP connections in a package’s{" "}
            <code>mcp.json</code>.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Try session Skills</h2>
      <p>
        Place a package directory containing <code>plugin.json</code> under
        <code>$VEASEL_DATA_DIR/plugins</code>, or set
        <code>VEASEL_PLUGIN_DIR</code> to another directory. Start the local
        backend and open a session in the TUI.
      </p>
      <pre><code>/skills
/skill on review-tools/review
/skill off review-tools/review</code></pre>
      <p>
        The plugin and Skill names come from the validated package manifest and
        directory. Selection persists with the session and is removed from the
        next model turn when disabled. Skill text cannot authorize tools or
        override Veasel’s security policy.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>What Veasel must add as the host</h2>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>
          A managed plugin registry with source, version, enabled state, and
          persistent per-install data.
        </li>
        <li>
          Isolated failure handling for malformed packages, unsupported
          components, and disconnected servers.
        </li>
        <li>
          Explicit trust decisions and exact tool approvals; plugin metadata
          cannot grant itself authority.
        </li>
        <li>
          Bounded MCP transports, cancellation, shutdown, and output limits.
        </li>
        <li>
          Skills and MCP tools integrated into the model loop and reviewed in
          the TUI.
        </li>
        <li>
          Specification-derived fixtures and a published compatibility report
          before claiming conformance.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        An <code>allowed-tools</code> hint in a Skill is advisory input. It must
        never override user policy or Veasel’s permission checks.
      </p>
    </div>
    <aside class="callout">
      <strong>Why compatibility is more than parsing.</strong> A plugin may
      start a local process or send data to a remote endpoint. Veasel must make
      those effects visible and keep them inside user-approved boundaries.
    </aside>
    <p class="doc-next">
      <a class="text-link" href="https://agent-plugins.org/specification">
        Read the Agent Plugins specification <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <a
        class="text-link"
        href="https://github.com/veasel-labs/veasel/blob/main/docs/compatibility/agent-plugins.md"
      >
        Review the conformance matrix <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${basePath}/docs/security/`}>
        See the security boundary <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
